---
layout: base
title: Sky over Ahvantir
description: "Solara, Nystara and the three moons placed for any hour of any day, as seen from Aru'Mas."
permalink: /almanac/sky/
---

<div class="page-header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1 class="page-title">Sky over Ahvantir</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">Solara, Nystara and the three moons placed for any hour of any day, seen from Aru&rsquo;Mas. Drag the sky to turn, scrub the hour, or set it running.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sky">

  {# Same pattern as the almanac ribbon and the relationship graph: a canvas
     cannot be navigated by tabbing through it, so it is one tab stop that
     listens for keys, and the keys are written down where a reader will find
     them rather than left to be guessed. #}
  <p class="alm-hint" id="sky-keys">
    Tab to the sky, then use the left and right arrows to turn, the up and down
    arrows to move the clock by a quarter hour, Page&nbsp;Up and Page&nbsp;Down to
    change the day, Home to face north, and Space to start or stop. Hold Shift to
    move in larger steps. Every control below is reachable on its own.
  </p>

  <section class="sky-stage" aria-labelledby="sky-stage-head">
    <h2 class="sr-only" id="sky-stage-head">The sky at the selected moment</h2>
    <div class="sky-box" id="sky-box">
      <canvas id="sky-canvas" class="sky-canvas" tabindex="0" role="application"
              aria-describedby="sky-keys"
              aria-label="The sky over Aru'Mas at the selected day and hour"></canvas>
      <div class="sky-hud">
        <div class="sky-hud__date" id="sky-hud-date"></div>
        <div class="sky-hud__state" id="sky-hud-state"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    {# The view buttons live in the controls, not over the canvas. On a phone
       there is no room for them beside the date and they sat on top of it; a
       wide screen has the room, so a media query lifts them back over the
       top-right corner of the sky. DOM order stays the same either way, which
       keeps the tab order sensible. #}
    <div class="sky-controls">
      <div class="sky-viewbtns">
        <button type="button" id="sky-view-pan" aria-pressed="true">Horizon</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-view-dome" aria-pressed="false">Dome</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-n" aria-label="Face north">N</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-e" aria-label="Face east">E</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-s" aria-label="Face south">S</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-w" aria-label="Face west">W</button>
      </div>

      <div class="sky-row">
        <div class="sky-field">
          <label for="sky-year">Year</label>
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-prev-year" aria-label="Previous year">&lsaquo;</button>
          <input id="sky-year" class="sky-ctl" type="number" min="0" max="9999" value="439" inputmode="numeric">
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-next-year" aria-label="Next year">&rsaquo;</button>
          <span class="sky-num">MC</span>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field">
          <label for="sky-month">Month</label>
          <select class="sky-ctl" id="sky-month"></select>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field">
          <label for="sky-day">Day</label>
          <select class="sky-ctl" id="sky-day"></select>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field">
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-prev-day">&lsaquo; Day</button>
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-next-day">Day &rsaquo;</button>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field sky-field--right">
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-play" aria-pressed="false">Play</button>
          <label for="sky-speed">Speed</label>
          <select class="sky-ctl" id="sky-speed">
            <option value="10">10 min / sec</option>
            <option value="60" selected>1 hour / sec</option>
            <option value="day">1 day / sec</option>
          </select>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sky-timeline">
        <span class="sky-clock" id="sky-clock">00:00</span>
        <div class="sky-slider">
          <div class="sky-band" id="sky-band" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <input id="sky-hour" class="sky-hour" type="range" min="0" max="23.983" step="0.0167"
                 aria-label="Hour of day">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sky-ticks" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>00</span><span>03</span><span>06</span><span>09</span><span>12</span>
        <span>15</span><span>18</span><span>21</span><span>24</span>
      </div>
      <p class="sr-only" id="sky-status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sky-grid">
    <div class="alm-panel">
      <div>
        <p class="alm-eyebrow">Ephemeris for the day</p>
        <h2 class="alm-dayhead__title" id="sky-eph-head"></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sky-pills" id="sky-pills"></div>
      <div class="sky-tablewrap">
        <table class="sky-table">
          <caption class="sr-only">Where each body stands now, when it rises and sets, and its phase</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Body</th><th scope="col">Now</th><th scope="col">Rises</th><th scope="col">Sets</th><th scope="col">Phase</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody id="sky-eph"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="alm-note">Moon phase names follow the Foundry phase tables, so they match the <a href="/almanac/" class="wikilink">Sky Almanac</a> and the table&rsquo;s own moon widget. The Pale Hour is Nystara in the sky with Solara below it &mdash; named for the light, not the length, so it can run to minutes or to most of two hours. Solara alone is simply the warm light, which has no other name.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="alm-panel sky-dialwrap">
      <div style="align-self: stretch">
        <p class="alm-eyebrow">Gnomon at the selected hour</p>
        <h2 class="alm-dayhead__title">Two Shadows</h2>
      </div>
      <canvas id="sky-dial" class="sky-dial" width="520" height="520"
              aria-label="A post and the two shadows it casts"></canvas>
      <p class="alm-note" id="sky-dial-note" style="align-self: stretch" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <p class="alm-note" style="align-self: stretch">Every lit object in Ahvantir casts two shadows &mdash; one warm and soft from Solara, one sharper and edged toward pale blue from Nystara. Where they overlap the shade is deeper and cooler than either alone.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <details class="sky-model">
    <summary>How this sky is modelled</summary>
    <div class="sky-model__body">
      <p><strong>From canon.</strong> The planet circles both suns on a circumbinary orbit. Solara is the larger, warm yellow disc; Nystara is smaller, blue-white and hotter. They orbit each other on roughly eight Marducian years, the first recorded Pairing in 0&nbsp;MC, and neither ever passes in front of the other &mdash; the Pairing is a convergence, not an eclipse. Moon cycles, phase tables and the Year&nbsp;1 moon epoch come from the Foundry calendar, and the day runs to 24 hours as Foundry runs it.</p>
      <p><strong>Working assumptions.</strong> These are invented to make a picture and are not settled lore. They are adjustable here so you can see what they change:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The observer stands in Aru&rsquo;Mas at the latitude below. Day length and how high the suns climb both depend on it.</li>
        <li>The longest day falls in mid-Cindralis and the longest night on Noctharis, matching the month descriptions.</li>
        <li>At their widest the suns stand about 19&deg; apart. Canon says only that there is &ldquo;a clear gap&rdquo;; this number was chosen because it gives a longest Pale Hour of just over an hour and three quarters, which is the &ldquo;nearly two hours&rdquo; the lore describes.</li>
        <li>At the Pairing they close to 0.8&deg;, which <em>is</em> pinned by canon, between the two statements that neither disc ever covers the other and that they end up &ldquo;separated by less than a sun-width&rdquo;. Expect that to show in the figures: in the years around a Pairing the two suns reach the horizon almost together, and the Pale Hour shrinks to minutes. It is named for its light rather than its length, so a short one is still a Pale Hour.</li>
        <li>Moon sizes and orbital tilts are invented, so a moon seen crossing a sun&rsquo;s face here is a result of these numbers and not a lore event.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="sky-model__ctl">
        <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-lat">Latitude</label><input class="sky-ctl" id="sky-lat" type="number" min="-80" max="80" step="1">&deg;</span>
        <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-tilt">Axial tilt</label><input class="sky-ctl" id="sky-tilt" type="number" min="0" max="60" step="1">&deg;</span>
        <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-spread">Widest sun spread</label><input class="sky-ctl" id="sky-spread" type="number" min="2" max="40" step="1">&deg;</span>
        <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-big">Enlarge discs</label><input id="sky-big" type="checkbox" checked></span>
      </div>
      <p class="alm-note">Discs are drawn three times their modelled size unless &ldquo;Enlarge discs&rdquo; is off. At true scale they are specks, which is accurate and useless.</p>
      <div class="sky-field">
        <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-share">Link to this sky</button>
        <input class="sky-share-out" id="sky-share-out" type="text" readonly hidden
               aria-label="Link to the sky currently shown">
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <section class="alm-notes" aria-labelledby="sky-notes-head">
    <p class="alm-eyebrow">Where this comes from</p>
    <h2 id="sky-notes-head">Sources</h2>
    <p>The calendar is the <a href="/articles/marducian-calendar/" class="wikilink">Marducian Calendar</a>; the suns, the Pairing, the two shadows and the Pale Hour are all from <a href="/articles/solara-and-nystara/" class="wikilink">Solara and Nystara</a>. For the year as a whole &mdash; every day&rsquo;s moon phases, the convergences and the dark skies &mdash; see the <a href="/almanac/" class="wikilink">Sky Almanac</a>.</p>
  </section>

</div>

{% block scripts %}
<script src="/assets/js/marducian.js"></script>
<script src="/assets/js/sky.js"></script>
{% endblock %}
